NAME
    make - rebuild commands from source, on-target

SYNOPSIS
    make [target]

DESCRIPTION
    A scaled-down make: it runs the pre-generated build script
    /src/mk/<target>.sh through the same engine as `sh`. make is an OS
    built-in. The .sh suffix is appended automatically (P8X shell
    scripts end in .sh), so `make dir` runs /src/mk/dir.sh.

    There are no file timestamps yet (P8XFS has no mtimes), so make
    ALWAYS rebuilds -- there is no up-to-date check. Dependency
    checking can be added later.

    Targets (the scripts under /src/mk, laid down by run.sh):
      make NAME       rebuild one command (both its C and asm twins)
      make c          rebuild every C command   -> /src/commands/c/bin
      make asm        rebuild every asm command -> /src/commands/asm/bin
      make            rebuild everything (same as `make all`)
      make installc   publish the C builds:   cp /src/commands/c/bin/*.bin   /bin
      make installa   publish the asm builds: cp /src/commands/asm/bin/*.bin /bin
      make os-bios    assemble the monitor + OS from /src/os-bios/asm

    Each target NAME maps to the script /src/mk/NAME.sh.

    A C command is built cc -> asm from /src/commands/c/NAME.c; a
    hand-asm command is assembled from /src/commands/asm/NAME.asm.
    The binaries land under /src/commands/{c,asm}/bin. Nothing is live
    until you install it: `make dir` then `make installc` (or
    `make installa`) copies the chosen twin over /bin.

EXAMPLES
    make pwd
    make dir
    make installc
    make

SEE ALSO
    sh, cc, asm, run
